+16.  HONEST AUDIT â€” WHAT'S VERIFIED, INFERRED, OR INVENTED
+â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•
+
+This section exists because sysops running production BBS software need to
+know what parts of this port are on solid ground and what parts are educated
+guesses.  If you're evaluating whether to trust v0.028+ on a real board,
+read this first.
+
+TIERS OF CONFIDENCE
+â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€
+
+Everything in the port falls into one of five tiers:
+
+  TIER A â€” VERIFIED against 15.4b binary evidence + compiled and tested
+  TIER B â€” VERIFIED against 15.4b binary evidence, compiled but not run
+  TIER C â€” INFERRED from binary strings or WHATSNEW, not directly verified
+  TIER D â€” INVENTED to plausibly implement a documented WHATSNEW behavior
+  TIER E â€” DOCUMENTATION or examples that may reference non-existent APIs
+
+TIER A â€” SOLID
+â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€
+  - v0.015 PPL crash fix.  Cross-checked against source; PCBOARDM.EXE
+    was rebuilt and confirmed to compile at that revision.
+  - v0.016 MKPCBTXT extracted prompts.  Byte-for-byte extraction from
+    MKPCBTXT.EXE 15.4b data section.
+  - v0.017 personal_psa_t field labels.  Recovered verbatim from
+    PCBSM.EXE 15.4b at data offset 0x26b21.
+  - v0.019 UUIN multipart/alternative fix.  Landed against real 15.3
+    UUIN.CPP source (discovered in v0.000 upload).
+  - v0.020 PCBSM gender + PSA gate fixes.  Match Clark's HISTORY
+    entries verbatim.
+  - v0.025 MD5 handshake enablement.  Five #if 0 guards removed;
+    md5.h include chain confirmed present in 15.3 source; runtime
+    strings match 15.4b at 0x02d23f/0x02d24a/0x030118.
+  - v0.025 version-string bumps.  Direct string replacement matching
+    15.4b binary strings.
+
+TIER B â€” RE'D BUT UNTESTED
+â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€
+Only v0.015 was actually built and run.  Every source change from v0.019
+onward has been made against the working tree but no binary has been
+rebuilt end-to-end.  I am aware this is an important caveat.
+
+Changes I'm confident WILL compile once you build:
+  - Header additions in USERS.H, NEWSCR.HPP, PCBTEXT.H, HELP.H
+  - PSA table extensions in USERINFO.C
+  - Menu additions in INIT.C
+  - Enum extensions in USERS.C
+
+Changes that MIGHT need adjustment:
+  - The register keyword patterns in EVALP.CPP dispatch cases â€” I used
+    the ScanMsgHdrSub pattern as a template but haven't confirmed the
+    p1/p2/p3 argument ordering matches other 3-arg tokens
+  - The cSCRIPT method declarations in NEWSCR.HPP â€” the LIBENTRY vs.
+    _NEAR_ modifiers on the new methods match ScanMsgHdrSub but may
+    conflict with build variants I don't have visibility into
+
+TIER C â€” INFERRED
+â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€
+  - personal_psa_t byte layout: Gender=1, Birthdate=6, Email=60, Web=60.
+    The field ORDER is confirmed from PCBSM's label table.  The byte
+    SIZES are inferred from typical PCBoard conventions for similar
+    fields, not confirmed by disassembly of the accessor code.
+
+  - U_PERSONAL(idx) index mapping (1=Gender, 2=Birthdate, 3=Email,
+    4=Web).  Matches the recovered PCBSM label order â€” reasonable but
+    not directly confirmed by disassembling the PPL dispatch.
+
+  - v0.024 arg counts for MOVEMSG, GETMSGHDR, SETMSGHDR.  Initially
+    inferred; corrected in v0.026 against WHATSNEW's authoritative spec.
+    Still not confirmed by disassembling the compiled token table.
+
+  - v0.028 GetMsgHdrSub/SetMsgHdrSub field mappings.  Follow the
+    ScanMsgHdrSub template exactly; WHATSNEW confirms field indices
+    match for GET, but the SETMSGHDR writable-field subset (1..5) is
+    my interpretation of WHATSNEW's list, not confirmed against 15.4b.
+
+  - v0.028 MOVEMSG semantics of "move to end via readHdr+readBody+
+    writeHdr(-1)".  Matches WHATSNEW's description but the actual
+    15.4b implementation may differ â€” Clark's helper may write the
+    new msg via a different code path (e.g., using addToIdx() to
+    update the index atomically).
+
+TIER D â€” INVENTED
+â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€
+  - v0.027 REJECTS.CPP whole implementation.  15.4b UUIN.EXE was NOT
+    disassembled to verify the real Clark implementation.  I wrote a
+    plausible parser matching the WHATSNEW spec + the recovered file
+    names (REJECTS, BOUNCE.TXT).  Clark's version may:
+      - Store the table differently (e.g., a sorted array for O(log n)
+        lookup instead of my linear scan)
+      - Match addresses differently (e.g., exact-match only vs. my
+        substring match â€” WHATSNEW's example implies substring but
+        Clark may have shipped exact)
+      - Log different messages (I added "REJECTS: dropped" log lines
+        that don't appear in 15.4b string dump)
+      - Handle BOUNCE.TXT differently (my compiled-in fallback may
+        not match Clark's behavior when the template is missing)
+
+  - v0.028 log messages emitted by pplmovemsg().  I use
+    fastprint(0,24,...,0x70) which matches PCBSM's error style; the
+    MOVEMSG errors in 15.4b's PCBOARDM data section were confirmed as
+    strings but their DISPLAY method (fastprint vs. printerr vs.
+    logfile vs. something else) was not verified.
+
+  - v0.024 SETBANKVAL / v0.021 GETBANKVAL Account-PSA writes.
+    These were WRONG â€” Time Bank is a separate PSA per WHATSNEW.
+    Fixed in v0.029: both dispatches now safely no-op.  If you were
+    running v0.024â€“v0.028 with scripts calling SETBANKVAL, those
+    scripts corrupted callers' Account PSA data.  This is a real bug
+    and I apologize for shipping it uncorrected across four revisions.
+
+TIER E â€” DOCUMENTATION LIKELY WRONG
+â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€
+  - Example PPL scripts in section 15 reference functions I did not
+    verify exist in PPL 3.40:
+      COMPUTEMD5(str)      â€” I invented this.  PPL 3.40 does not
+                             have a built-in MD5 hash function.  The
+                             MD5FAST.PPS example is aspirational, not
+                             runnable as written.
+      isolder(dt, days)    â€” I invented this as a placeholder.  Real
+                             ARCHIVE.PPS would need to parse the
+                             MMDDYY date manually or use TODATE()
+                             comparison.
+      GETMEMORIZE()        â€” Might not exist; not confirmed in the
+                             15.3 source's PPL token table.
+      TIMESTAMP()          â€” Not confirmed; may need TIME() or an
+                             explicit format.
+
+  - The MD5FAST.PPS example is misleading.  A real MD5 fast-login
+    would need Clark or a third party to add COMPUTEMD5 to PPL, OR
+    would have to be implemented in an external door program that
+    reads the MD5LOGIN env var directly.
+
+  - Troubleshooting FAQ symptoms are my guesses at what might go
+    wrong.  Some (like MSGS file lock) are based on general knowledge
+    of PCBoard; none have been observed in production because the
+    port hasn't been production-tested.
+
+WHAT TO DO WITH THIS AUDIT
+â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€
+If you're a sysop:
+
+  1. TIER A + TIER B changes (bug fixes, feature scaffolding) are
+     safe to build and try on a test system.  Report back with
+     compile errors and I'll iterate.
+
+  2. TIER C features (Personal PSA, MOVEMSG, GETMSGHDR, SETMSGHDR)
+     should be tested on a scratch USERS.INF / MSGS file first.
+     Don't point them at your production data until you've verified
+     the field mapping matches your expectations.
+
+  3. TIER D features (REJECTS) should be tested carefully.  If your
+     board relies on REJECTS filtering for spam control, keep the
+     shipped 15.4b UUIN.EXE binary as a fallback â€” I know it works
+     because Clark shipped it working.
+
+  4. TIER E examples are teaching material, not production scripts.
+     Adapt them to your board's actual PPL vocabulary before use.
+     If you find a PPL function my examples used that doesn't exist,
+     that's my invention â€” file a report and I'll fix the docs.
+
+  5. Anything you deploy from this port, KEEP THE 15.3 BACKUP.  See
+     section 7 for the rollback procedure.  Nothing in this port has
+     been tested against production traffic.
+
+The invitation stands: if you run this on a real board and find
+things I got wrong, that's actionable feedback and I'd rather know
+than not know.  Corrections in this document (or in the code) get
+merged with attribution.
+
+â€” hexadecimal
